Notebook[{Cell["Getting Started with MagneticTB", "Title"], Cell["To construct a tight-binding model, first specify the lattice, atomic positions, symmetry operations, and local orbitals. With these inputs, MagneticTB finds the equivalent atoms and the symmetry-allowed hopping terms. We use graphene to show how to obtain the Hamiltonian and plot its bands.", "Text"], Cell["Install, update, uninstall, and load", "Section"], Cell["Obtain a local MagneticTB .paclet file, either supplied to you or built from the source. The installation commands below use this file; they do not download a release from the Internet.", "Text"], Cell["Install", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{ButtonBox["MagneticTB help home", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:MagneticTB/guide/MagneticTB"]}], "Text"], Cell["Install the .paclet file with the following command. Replace the path and filename with those of your file:", "Text"], Cell[BoxData["PacletInstall[\"/absolute/path/MagneticTB-2.x.y.paclet\"]"], "Input"], Cell["After installation, quit Mathematica completely and open it again before loading MagneticTB or opening its help. This also removes any definitions left by the previously loaded version.", "Text"], Cell["Update", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{ButtonBox["MagneticTB help home", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:MagneticTB/guide/MagneticTB"]}], "Text"], Cell["To update, install the newer .paclet file in the same way, then quit and reopen Mathematica. If PacletInstall reports samevers, that version is already installed. To replace it with another file of the same version, uninstall that version first and then install the new file.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData["PacletInstall[\"/absolute/path/MagneticTB-2.x.y.paclet\"]"], "Input"], Cell["Uninstall or reinstall the same version", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{ButtonBox["MagneticTB help home", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:MagneticTB/guide/MagneticTB"]}], "Text"], Cell["To uninstall MagneticTB, run the following command, then quit and reopen Mathematica:", "Text"], Cell[BoxData["PacletUninstall[\"MagneticTB\"]"], "Input"], Cell["Load and open the documentation", "Subsection"], Cell[TextData[{ButtonBox["MagneticTB help home", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:MagneticTB/guide/MagneticTB"]}], "Text"], Cell["After reopening Mathematica, load the package with Needs. To open the help, search for MagneticTB in the Documentation Center, or run the command below to open its home page. The functions from version 1.0 are loaded with MagneticTBOld`; use a separate kernel for that version.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData["Needs[\"MagneticTB`\"]"], "Input"], Cell[BoxData["SystemOpen[\"paclet:MagneticTB/guide/MagneticTB\"]"], "Input"], Cell["The five physical inputs", "Section"], Cell["init sets up the crystal and its local orbitals. Here lattice gives the three lattice vectors as rows, and lattpar supplies their parameter values. wyckoffposition gives one representative position and its magnetic moment for each inequivalent Wyckoff orbit. symminformation gives the symmetry operations, and basisFunctions lists the orbitals in the same order as the positions.", "Text"], Cell["The two carbon atoms in graphene are symmetry equivalent. Enter only {1/3,2/3,0}; the other atom at {2/3,1/3,0} is generated by symmetry. Keep one pz orbital on each carbon. The lattice parameter c separates the periodically repeated graphene layers.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[GridBox[{{Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "lattice", Cell["length", "TableText"], Cell["Three primitive vectors stored as rows. Coordinates in wyckoffposition are fractional coordinates in this basis.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "lattpar", Cell["length", "TableText"], Cell["Numerical assignments for every symbolic lattice constant. MagneticTB does not impose an angstrom unit; all lengths must use one consistent unit.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "wyckoffposition", Cell["fractional position and magnetic moment", "TableText"], Cell["The outer list enumerates inequivalent Wyckoff orbits. Each entry is {seed position, magnetic moment}; the same outer order is used by basisFunctions.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "symminformation", Cell["symmetry operations", "TableText"], Cell["Each magnetic-group record is {label,R,t,\"F\"|\"T\"}. The last field records whether the operation is unitary or antiunitary.", "TableText"]}, {Cell["      ", "TableRowIcon"], "basisFunctions", Cell["local orbital basis", "TableText"], Cell["One nonempty local basis list per Wyckoff orbit. Different entries may have different dimensions, which produces rectangular inter-orbit hopping blocks.", "TableText"]}}, GridBoxAlignment -> {"Columns" -> {{Left}}}, GridBoxDividers -> {"Rows" -> {{False}}}, GridBoxSpacings -> {"Columns" -> {Offset[0.27999999999999997], {Offset[0.7]}, Offset[0.27999999999999997]}, "Rows" -> {Offset[0.2], {Offset[0.4]}, Offset[0.2]}}]], "3ColumnTableMod"], Cell[BoxData["grapheneOperations = msgop[gray[191]];\ninit[\n  lattice -> {\n    {a, 0, 0},\n    {-a/2, Sqrt[3] a/2, 0},\n    {0, 0, c}\n  },\n  lattpar -> {a -> 1, c -> 3},\n  wyckoffposition -> {\n    {{1/3, 2/3, 0}, {0, 0, 0}}\n  },\n  symminformation -> grapheneOperations,\n  basisFunctions -> {{\"pz\"}}\n];"], "Input"], Cell["View atomic positions and magnetization directions", "Section"], Cell["After init, use atompos to view the generated atomic positions and magnetization directions. The atoms are grouped by Wyckoff orbit. Each entry is {fractional position, magnetization direction}, with both vectors expressed in the lattice-vector basis:", "Text"], Cell[BoxData["atompos"], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "0", ",", "0"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"0", ",", "0", ",", "0"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}], "}"}]], "Output"], Cell["The two entries are the carbon atoms at {1/3,2/3,0} and {2/3,1/3,0}. Their magnetization directions are both {0,0,0}, since this model is nonmagnetic.", "Text"], Cell["Check the neighbour shells", "Section"], Cell["Hoppings are numbered by distance. Number 1 is the onsite term, number 2 is the nearest neighbor, and number 3 is the second neighbor. For graphene, showbonds[2] displays the three nearest-neighbor carbon-carbon bonds and their reverse directions. No hopping parameters need to be specified to draw these bonds.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData["showbonds[2]"], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[TagBox[GridBox[{{TemplateBox[{"\"Bond shell \"", "2", "\" (\"", "1", "\"-th neighbour), \"", "\"bond length = \"", "0.5773502691896257`", "\"; \"", "6", "\" directed bonds, \"", "1", "\" directed orbits\""}, "RowDefault"], "", "", ""}, {"\"Site index\"", "\"Atom position (fractional)\"", "\"Num. of directed bonds\"", TemplateBox[{TagBox[SubsuperscriptBox["p", "k", "'"], DisplayForm], "\" (fractional)\""}, "RowDefault"]}, {"1", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], "3", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", FractionBox["1", "3"]}], ",", FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", FractionBox["4", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}, {"2", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], "3", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", RowBox[{"-", FractionBox["1", "3"]}], ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["1", "3"], ",", FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["4", "3"], ",", FractionBox["2", "3"], ",", "0"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}}, GridBoxAlignment -> {"Columns" -> {{Left}}}, AutoDelete -> False, GridBoxFrame -> {"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}}, GridBoxItemSize -> {"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}}], "Grid"]], "Output"], Cell["Solve the Hamiltonian shell by shell", "Section"], Cell["First calculate the onsite term with symham[1]. The two carbon atoms are equivalent, so both diagonal entries contain the same real parameter e1:", "Text"], Cell[BoxData["grapheneOnsite = symham[1];\nMatrixForm[grapheneOnsite]"], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[TagBox[RowBox[{"(", "⁠", GridBox[{{"e1", "0"}, {"0", "e1"}}, RowSpacings -> 1, ColumnSpacings -> 1, RowAlignments -> Baseline, ColumnAlignments -> Center], "⁠", ")"}], Function[BoxForm`e$, MatrixForm[BoxForm`e$]]]], "Output"], Cell["Next calculate the nearest-neighbor hopping with symham[2]. The three phase factors in the upper-right entry come from the three nearest-neighbor bonds. For real t1, the lower-left entry is its complex conjugate:", "Text"], Cell[BoxData["grapheneNearest = symham[2];\nMatrixForm[grapheneNearest]"], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[TagBox[RowBox[{"(", "⁠", GridBox[{{"0", RowBox[{RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["", RowBox[{"", " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", FractionBox[RowBox[{"2", " ", "kx"}], "3"]}], "-", FractionBox["ky", "3"]}], ")"}]}]], " ", "t1"}], "+", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["", RowBox[{"", " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{FractionBox["kx", "3"], "-", FractionBox["ky", "3"]}], ")"}]}]], " ", "t1"}], "+", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["", RowBox[{"", " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{FractionBox["kx", "3"], "+", FractionBox[RowBox[{"2", " ", "ky"}], "3"]}], ")"}]}]], " ", "t1"}]}]}, {RowBox[{RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["", RowBox[{"", " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", FractionBox["kx", "3"]}], "-", FractionBox[RowBox[{"2", " ", "ky"}], "3"]}], ")"}]}]], " ", "t1"}], "+", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["", RowBox[{"", " ", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", FractionBox["kx", "3"]}], "+", FractionBox["ky", "3"]}], ")"}]}]], " ", 